   "width_basis": "water_width_m = 46 (cited, see width_today_sources), centred on the traced centre line (lidar-today); water edges inside the flat band could not be found: every cell in the band reads 3.40 m to within 0.02 m (checked on 10 rows), so the DEM does not show where the water ends and the flat bank begins. The flat band (mean 99 m across) is water plus towpath/bank ground; the strip between flat-band edge and water edge is bank ground (feature canal_bank_ground).",
   "source_tag": "lidar-today",
   "note": "Derived from RGE ALTI 1 m, not typed. Method: each 20 m row (y -380..600) of the grid is scanned for runs of cells whose height is within 0.06 m of canal water level (3.40 m NGF = z 0); runs shorter than 30 m are dropped (isolated flat patches); the canal is then tracked from the bridge row (x -6..68 at y 0) north and south, taking at each row the run that overlaps the previous row's run (within 30 m drift). That tracking separates the canal from other flat ground: the Orne and its flood plain lie 500 m or more to the east and read below 0 m or are not flat to 0.06 m, marsh and fields are not flat to 0.06 m, and any flat patch beyond the tracked run has no continuous link to it. Banks are the run ends (cell edges). The canal runs 28.3 deg east of grid north. Caveat: 'flat at water level' is canal water plus any equally flat strip next to it, so the band may be wider than today's water and than the 1944 canal; the 1944 width is not derived from it. ROUND 2: water width is now 46 m (cited estimate) centred on this centre line; the 99 m mean band is kept as flat_band_*.",
